{"seconds": 217.5, "exit": 0, "final": "**CHECKMATE!!!**\n\nSo the line is:\n1.Qf3+ Kh4 (forced)\n2.Qg4# mate\n\nPerfect! So **Qf3+** is the best move!\n\nLet me verify the main line one more time to make sure. After Qf3+, Black's only options that don't lose are:\n- Kh4 leads to Qg4# mate\n- Kh6 and Kg5 lead to continuing checks and lost position\n- Rxf3 loses the rook and White captures with gxf3, winning material\n\nSo Kh4 is Black's \"best\" try but it leads to mate.\n\n**Answer: Qf3+**\n\n**Main line:** Qf3+ Kh4 Qg4# (mate)\n\n**Motif:** attraction (the forcing check draws the king to h4 where it's mated)\n\nHere's the coaching note:\n\n---\n\n**Coaching Note (Club Player, ~1500 rating):**\n\nYour opponent's king was trapped on h5 by its own pawns. The winning move forced it to move with check\u2014**Qf3+** attacks the rook and checks the king simultaneously. When the king runs to h4 (the only move that doesn't lose material), it walks into a mating net: **Qg4#** is checkmate because the queen controls all escape squares (g3, g5, h5, and h3 is blocked by White's pawn). \n\n**Key lesson:** When the enemy king is confined by its own pieces, use forcing moves (checks and threats) to drive it into a tighter corner. Look for moves that attack multiple targets at once\u2014here, the check and the attack on the rook gave Black no good options. Next time you see a trapped king, look for forcing moves that narrow its escape routes.\n\n---\n\n```json\n{\"move\": \"Qf3+\", \"line\": [\"Qf3+\", \"Kh4\", \"Qg4#\"], \"motifs\": [\"attraction\", \"fork\"]}\n```", "tool_calls": [{"tool": "skill", "args": {"skill": "chess-coach"}}, {"tool": "chess-eyes-board", "args": {"fen": "5Q2/7p/6p1/1p2q2k/2p5/P1P1r2P/6P1/6RK w - - 6 47"}}, {"tool": "chess-eyes-threats", "args": {"fen": "5Q2/7p/6p1/1p2q2k/2p5/P1P1r2P/6P1/6RK w - - 6 47"}}, {"tool": "chess-eyes-line_view", "args": {"fen": "5Q2/7p/6p1/1p2q2k/2p5/P1P1r2P/6P1/6RK w - - 6 47", "square": "h5"}}, {"tool": "chess-eyes-line_view", 